# The fs module: a module of GCSL's standard library, compiled into every
# build and loaded by a run that imports it (`import fs`). It touches the
# file system, so every member is IMPURE, called from a `->` or `->>`
# function, a `lazy` or a top level, never from a pure function; and every
# path it takes must be ABSOLUTE, used as given, never normalised and never
# against the working directory (a relative one is a runtime error, as
# `fs.read("x.txt")` is): join one with `path.join(fileroot.this, "x.txt")`.
# Its members are the built-in functions of the file system themselves,
# bound here as the prelude binds the others, so a failure is reported at
# the call that made it, naming the member, `fs.read`, by whatever name it
# was imported as:
#
# - `exists(p)`: whether something is at `p`, symbolic links followed;
# - `read(p)`: the contents of the file at `p`, which must be UTF-8 text;
# - `mkdir(p)`: makes the directory `p` and any missing parents, as
#   `mkdir -p`; one already there is fine;
# - `copy(from, to)`: copies the file `from`, its contents and permissions,
#   to `to`, whose directory must exist, replacing a file there;
# - `set_executable(p, executable)`: makes the file `p` executable by
#   whoever can read it, or by no one if `executable` is false, as a
#   downloaded program needs. A file of several hard links shares its mode
#   with every other, so changing one is an error: copy it first;
# - `temp_dir()`: makes a new empty directory, mode 0700, and returns its
#   absolute path. Every directory it makes is removed, with everything in
#   it, when the run is done, whether it succeeded or failed (but not if
#   the process is killed);
# - `write(p, contents)`: writes `contents` to the file `p`, whose directory
#   must exist, making it or replacing it whole, ATOMICALLY: to a new
#   temporary file beside it, `.NAME.gcsl-tmp-PID-N`, renamed over `p`, so a
#   reader sees the old contents or the new, never a part, and a failure
#   leaves no temporary file behind. A file it replaces does not keep its
#   permissions: the new file has the mode any new file gets (0666 less the
#   umask), and a symbolic link at `p` is replaced, not followed. `p` a
#   directory is an error;
# - `compose_dir(entries)`: a new directory of the run, removed as
#   `temp_dir`'s are, of the record `entries`, whose every key is a path in
#   it, `/` or `.` for the directory itself: a `Dir`'s tree is copied under
#   its key, a `File` to its key, and each file of a `List<File>` under its
#   key by its name. Directories merge; two entries placing one file is an
#   error, as a key leaving the directory is. Everything is COPIED, its
#   permissions kept and its symbolic links as links, so that a program
#   writing in the directory changes nothing it was made of; a host that
#   isolates steps, as pekit does, mounts instead;
# - `compose(entries)`: `compose_dir`'s directory, but of the list
#   `entries`, each `{at = PATH; place = WHAT}`, so that a path can be
#   computed, `"include/{name}.h"`, as no record's key can.
#
# Like every standard file it is a scope of its own: it sees its own
# declarations, the prelude's names, and `builtins`.
import builtins

exists = builtins.fs_exists
read = builtins.fs_read
mkdir = builtins.fs_mkdir
copy = builtins.fs_copy
set_executable = builtins.fs_set_executable
temp_dir = builtins.fs_temp_dir
write = builtins.fs_write
compose_dir = builtins.fs_compose_dir
compose = builtins.fs_compose
